('BANKED_CLOZE',
 '<p>The concept of emotional intelligence has gained <b>1._____</b> attention in both academic and corporate circles. Unlike traditional measures of cognitive ability, emotional intelligence refers to the <b>2._____</b> to recognize, understand, and manage one own emotions as well as those of others. Research suggests that emotional intelligence may be a better <b>3._____</b> of professional success than IQ alone.</p><p>Individuals with high emotional intelligence tend to <b>4._____</b> in collaborative environments. They are skilled at navigating social <b>5._____</b> and resolving conflicts constructively. In leadership roles, emotional intelligence <b>6._____</b> managers to build stronger teams and foster more positive work cultures. Companies are increasingly <b>7._____</b> emotional intelligence assessments into their hiring and promotion processes.</p><p>However, some critics <b>8._____</b> that emotional intelligence is difficult to measure objectively and that its importance has been <b>9._____</b> by consultants selling assessment tools. Despite these concerns, the <b>10._____</b> of evidence suggests that emotional competencies play a significant role in personal and professional achievement.</p>',
 '[{"label":"A","text":"considerable"},{"label":"B","text":"capacity"},{"label":"C","text":"predictor"},{"label":"D","text":"excel"},{"label":"E","text":"dynamics"},{"label":"F","text":"enables"},{"label":"G","text":"incorporating"},{"label":"H","text":"contend"},{"label":"I","text":"exaggerated"},{"label":"J","text":"weight"},{"label":"K","text":"limited"},{"label":"L","text":"ability"},{"label":"M","text":"ignore"},{"label":"N","text":"rejected"},{"label":"O","text":"minimal"}]',
 'A,B,C,D,E,F,G,H,I,J',
 '1. considerable(相当大的) 2. capacity(能力) 3. predictor(预测指标) 4. excel(表现出色) 5. dynamics(动态关系) 6. enables(使能够) 7. incorporating(纳入) 8. contend(主张/认为) 9. exaggerated(夸大的) 10. weight(分量)',
 7, '选词填空,情商,职场'),

('BANKED_CLOZE',
 '<p>The rapid development of autonomous vehicle technology has the potential to <b>1._____</b> transform transportation systems worldwide. Proponents argue that self-driving cars will <b>2._____</b> traffic accidents, which currently claim over 1.3 million lives annually. They also promise to reduce congestion and provide <b>3._____</b> for elderly and disabled individuals who cannot drive themselves.</p><p>However, the transition to autonomous vehicles presents <b>4._____</b> challenges. The technology must be essentially perfect before widespread deployment, as public <b>5._____</b> could be severely damaged by high-profile accidents. Regulatory frameworks lag far behind technological development, creating legal <b>6._____</b> around liability when autonomous vehicles are involved in crashes.</p><p>The economic <b>7._____</b> of autonomous vehicles will be profound. Millions of professional drivers—truckers, taxi operators, delivery personnel—may find their livelihoods <b>8._____</b>. While new jobs will be created in technology development and fleet management, the transition is likely to be <b>9._____</b> for many workers. Policymakers face the challenge of managing this transition while <b>10._____</b> the safety and mobility benefits that autonomous vehicles promise.</p>',
 '[{"label":"A","text":"fundamentally"},{"label":"B","text":"reduce"},{"label":"C","text":"mobility"},{"label":"D","text":"formidable"},{"label":"E","text":"trust"},{"label":"F","text":"uncertainty"},{"label":"G","text":"implications"},{"label":"H","text":"disrupted"},{"label":"I","text":"painful"},{"label":"J","text":"maximizing"},{"label":"K","text":"slightly"},{"label":"L","text":"increase"},{"label":"M","text":"certainty"},{"label":"N","text":"protected"},{"label":"O","text":"ignored"}]',
 'A,B,C,D,E,F,G,H,I,J',
 '1. fundamentally(根本性地) 2. reduce(减少) 3. mobility(出行便利) 4. formidable(巨大的) 5. trust(信任) 6. uncertainty(不确定性) 7. implications(影响) 8. disrupted(被打乱) 9. painful(痛苦的) 10. maximizing(最大化)',
 7, '选词填空,自动驾驶,科技'),

-- ==================== 长篇匹配 7-10 ====================
('LONG_MATCH',
 '<p><b>[A]</b> The circular economy represents a fundamental departure from the traditional linear model of production and consumption. In the linear economy, resources are extracted, processed into products, used, and then discarded as waste. The circular economy, by contrast, aims to keep materials in use for as long as possible through strategies including reuse, repair, remanufacturing, and recycling.</p><p><b>[B]</b> The environmental benefits of circularity are substantial. The Ellen MacArthur Foundation estimates that adopting circular economy principles could reduce global greenhouse gas emissions by 45% by 2050. Much of this reduction would come from changes in how we produce and use materials like steel, aluminum, cement, and plastics—sectors that are notoriously difficult to decarbonize through energy transition alone.</p><p><b>[C]</b> Business interest in circular models has grown rapidly. Major corporations including IKEA, Philips, and Renault have announced circular economy strategies, recognizing both the environmental imperative and the economic opportunities. For some companies, circularity means shifting from selling products to providing services—for example, selling lighting as a service rather than selling light bulbs, which incentivizes the manufacturer to design for durability and easy maintenance.</p><p><b>[D]</b> The concept of planned obsolescence—designing products with deliberately limited lifespans to encourage repeat purchases—stands in direct opposition to circular economy principles. Critics have documented how manufacturers of electronics, appliances, and even clothing employ design strategies that make products difficult or impossible to repair. Right-to-repair legislation, enacted in several jurisdictions, seeks to counter this trend by requiring manufacturers to make repair information and parts available to consumers.</p><p><b>[E]</b> China has emerged as an unexpected leader in certain aspects of circular economy policy. The country 2008 Circular Economy Promotion Law established a comprehensive legal framework for resource efficiency and waste reduction. Chinese industrial parks have pioneered "industrial symbiosis," in which the waste outputs of one factory become the raw material inputs for another, creating closed-loop systems at the industrial scale.</p><p><b>[F]</b> The fashion industry, one of the world most polluting sectors, has become a focal point for circular economy advocates. Fast fashion business model, based on rapid turnover of inexpensive clothing, generates enormous textile waste. Emerging alternatives include clothing rental services, resale platforms, and brands designing garments specifically for disassembly and recycling. However, scaling these models to match the dominance of fast fashion remains a significant challenge.</p><p><b>[G]</b> Critics of the circular economy concept argue that it has been co-opted by corporations for greenwashing purposes. Weak interpretations of circularity, they contend, allow companies to claim environmental credentials while making only marginal changes to fundamentally unsustainable business models. True circularity, these critics argue, requires not just better recycling but a fundamental reduction in material consumption, particularly in wealthy nations.</p><p><b>[H]</b> The transition to a circular economy faces significant barriers, including existing infrastructure designed for linear systems, economic incentives that favor virgin materials over recycled ones, and consumer expectations shaped by decades of disposable culture. Overcoming these barriers will require coordinated action across government policy, business strategy, and consumer behavior—a challenge as complex as it is urgent.</p>',
 '[{"label":"1","text":"Fast fashion illustrates both the problem of textile waste and the potential for circular business models like rental and resale."},{"label":"2","text":"Some companies are changing from selling products to providing ongoing services to align with circular principles."},{"label":"3","text":"The existing infrastructure and economic incentives are designed for the linear model, making the transition difficult."},{"label":"4","text":"Critics say companies use circular economy language for marketing without making meaningful changes."},{"label":"5","text":"Reducing emissions from heavy industries like steel and cement is where circularity offers the greatest climate benefit."},{"label":"6","text":"Laws requiring manufacturers to share repair information are challenging the practice of planned obsolescence."},{"label":"7","text":"An Asian country has created a legal system where waste from one factory can serve as materials for another."},{"label":"8","text":"The circular model keeps resources circulating through reuse and recycling rather than discarding them."},{"label":"9","text":"China established a comprehensive national law on resource efficiency in the late 2000s."},{"label":"10","text":"The traditional linear economy follows a pattern of extracting resources, making products, using them, and throwing them away."}]',
 'F,C,H,G,B,D,E,A,E,A',
 '1→F(fast fashion) 2→C(lighting as service) 3→H(barriers) 4→G(greenwashing) 5→B(steel cement 45%) 6→D(right-to-repair) 7→E(industrial symbiosis) 8→A(circular economy def) 9→E(2008 law) 10→A(linear model)',
 7, '长篇匹配,循环经济,环保'),

('LONG_MATCH',
 '<p><b>[A]</b> The relationship between social media use and adolescent mental health has become one of the most contentious topics in public health. Rates of depression, anxiety, and self-harm among teenagers have risen sharply since roughly 2012, coinciding with the period when smartphone ownership and social media use became nearly universal among young people. Whether this correlation reflects causation, and if so through what mechanisms, remains hotly debated.</p><p><b>[B]</b> Several large-scale studies have found statistically significant associations between heavy social media use and poor mental health outcomes, particularly among adolescent girls. The proposed mechanisms include social comparison (comparing oneself unfavorably to others carefully curated online presentations), displacement of protective activities like sleep and exercise, and exposure to cyberbullying and harmful content. However, the effect sizes in most studies are modest, and some researchers argue they have been overstated in media coverage.</p><p><b>[C]</b> The debate intensified following the publication of a 2023 report by the US Surgeon General, which described the mental health crisis among young people as an urgent public health issue and pointed to social media as a contributing factor. The report called for stronger safety standards for social media platforms, increased research funding, and the development of digital literacy curricula in schools. It stopped short of recommending specific time limits but urged families to establish tech-free zones and times.</p><p><b>[D]</b> Not all research points in the same direction. Several longitudinal studies have found little or no evidence that social media use causes declines in well-being at the population level. Proponents of the "weak effects" position argue that the relationship between digital technology and mental health is complex and highly individual: some teenagers are negatively affected, some are positively affected, and most experience both benefits and harms.</p><p><b>[E]</b> The design of social media platforms has come under particular scrutiny. Features such as infinite scrolling, autoplay, and algorithmically curated content are designed to maximize engagement rather than user welfare. Some researchers argue that the problem is not "social media" in general but specific design choices that exploit psychological vulnerabilities, particularly among young users whose prefrontal cortex—the brain center for impulse control—is still developing.</p><p><b>[F]</b> Cross-national comparisons reveal interesting patterns. While concerns about social media and youth mental health are prominent in the US, UK, and Australia, they receive less attention in some European and Asian countries. This variation may reflect differences in platform usage patterns, cultural attitudes toward technology, or the robustness of alternative social support systems. It suggests that national context plays a significant role in mediating the effects of digital technology on well-being.</p><p><b>[G]</b> The policy response has been fragmented. Some jurisdictions have introduced age verification requirements or restrictions on social media use by minors. Utah became the first US state to require parental consent for minors to use social media platforms. However, critics argue that such measures are difficult to enforce and may have unintended consequences, such as driving young people to less regulated platforms.</p><p><b>[H]</b> The path forward likely lies in a nuanced approach that acknowledges both risks and benefits. Social media provides genuine value to many young people, facilitating social connection, identity exploration, and access to information. The challenge is to design platforms, policies, and educational approaches that maximize these benefits while protecting the most vulnerable users from demonstrable harms—a goal that requires cooperation from technology companies, which have thus far been reluctant to share data with independent researchers.</p>',
 '[{"label":"1","text":"Some research using long-term tracking of the same individuals found minimal evidence that social media harms well-being at the population level."},{"label":"2","text":"Specific interface features like endless content loading may exploit the not-yet-mature impulse control of adolescent brains."},{"label":"3","text":"The difficulty of enforcing age-based restrictions may push teenagers toward platforms with even fewer safety protections."},{"label":"4","text":"The increase in teen mental health problems starting around 2012 coincided with widespread smartphone adoption."},{"label":"5","text":"Technology companies have been unwilling to provide research access to their internal data."},{"label":"6","text":"A 2023 official US government report identified social media as a factor in the youth mental health crisis."},{"label":"7","text":"The severity of the problem may be smaller than what is commonly reported in news coverage."},{"label":"8","text":"Different countries show different levels of concern about youth social media use, suggesting cultural factors matter."},{"label":"9","text":"Comparing oneself unfavorably to the idealized lives presented online is one way social media may harm mental health."},{"label":"10","text":"One US state passed a law requiring minors to have parental permission before using social media."}]',
 'D,E,G,A,H,C,B,F,B,G',
 '1→D(weak effects) 2→E(prefrontal cortex) 3→G(unintended consequences) 4→A(2012 correlation) 5→H(reluctant data sharing) 6→C(Surgeon General) 7→B(modest effect sizes) 8→F(cross-national) 9→B(social comparison) 10→G(Utah)',
 7, '长篇匹配,社交媒体,心理健康'),

('LONG_MATCH',
 '<p><b>[A]</b> Remote work, once a niche practice limited to a small fraction of knowledge workers, became a global phenomenon almost overnight with the onset of the COVID-19 pandemic. What began as an emergency public health measure has evolved into a permanent restructuring of how and where work is performed, with profound implications for cities, corporations, and individual workers.</p><p><b>[B]</b> The productivity debate has been central to discussions about the future of remote work. Early in the pandemic, many companies reported that productivity remained stable or even increased after the shift to remote work. However, more recent studies present a more nuanced picture. While individual task completion often improves without office distractions, collaborative activities that depend on spontaneous interaction—brainstorming, mentoring, building organizational culture—appear to suffer in fully remote environments.</p><p><b>[C]</b> The geographical implications of remote work are potentially transformative. If knowledge workers are no longer tethered to expensive urban centers, they can relocate to smaller cities, suburbs, or rural areas offering lower costs of living and higher quality of life. This dispersion could reduce housing pressure in superstar cities like San Francisco and New York while revitalizing economically struggling regions. Some countries have introduced "digital nomad" visas specifically designed to attract remote workers.</p><p><b>[D]</b> The impact on commercial real estate has been dramatic. Office vacancy rates in major US cities reached record highs in 2023, and commercial property values have declined substantially. This has cascading effects on municipal budgets, which depend heavily on commercial property taxes, and on the ecosystem of businesses—restaurants, dry cleaners, transit systems—that serve office workers. Some urban economists warn of a "doom loop" in which declining transit ridership leads to service cuts, making downtowns less attractive, further eroding the office market.</p><p><b>[E]</b> Gender dynamics in remote work present a complex picture. On one hand, remote work can facilitate better work-life integration, potentially enabling more equitable sharing of domestic responsibilities. On the other hand, research suggests that women working remotely are more likely than men to be simultaneously managing childcare, creating a "double shift" that can exacerbate rather than alleviate gender inequality. The physical separation of work from home, for those who can achieve it, appears to provide clearer boundaries.</p><p><b>[F]</b> Organizational culture faces perhaps its greatest test in the remote work era. How do companies onboard new employees, transmit institutional knowledge, and build the social bonds that underpin effective collaboration when colleagues rarely or never meet in person? Some organizations have adopted hybrid models—requiring two or three days per week in the office—as a compromise solution. Others have gone fully remote, investing heavily in digital collaboration tools and periodic in-person gatherings.</p><p><b>[G]</b> The global dimension of remote work raises important questions about labor arbitrage. If a job can be performed from anywhere, why should employers pay San Francisco or London salaries when they can hire equally qualified workers in lower-cost countries? This dynamic, already visible in industries like software development and customer service, could put downward pressure on wages in high-income countries while creating new opportunities for workers in developing economies.</p><p><b>[H]</b> Looking ahead, the most likely outcome appears to be a hybrid model in which remote and in-office work coexist, with the specific balance varying by industry, organization, and individual role. The task for leaders is to design work arrangements that capture the benefits of flexibility—autonomy, reduced commuting, access to broader talent pools—while mitigating the costs in collaboration, culture, and equity. There is no single template; the most successful organizations will be those that approach the challenge with intentionality and a willingness to experiment.</p>',
 '[{"label":"1","text":"The possibility that companies could hire equally skilled workers in cheaper countries poses a threat to salaries in wealthy nations."},{"label":"2","text":"A potential cycle where fewer commuters leads to reduced transit, making city centers less desirable and further reducing office demand."},{"label":"3","text":"Remote work may allow women more flexibility, but evidence shows they often handle both work and childcare simultaneously."},{"label":"4","text":"While individual work tasks may improve remotely, spontaneous interactions that support creativity appear to decline."},{"label":"5","text":"Hybrid approaches with some in-office days have emerged as a compromise between fully remote and fully in-person work."},{"label":"6","text":"The most successful future organizations will intentionally experiment rather than following a single prescribed model."},{"label":"7","text":"Knowledge workers relocating from expensive cities could help revitalize declining regions while easing urban housing costs."},{"label":"8","text":"The pandemic transformed remote work from a rare arrangement into a mainstream practice almost instantly."},{"label":"9","text":"Transmitting organizational knowledge and integrating new employees is difficult when colleagues never meet face to face."},{"label":"10","text":"Some nations have created special visa categories to attract people who can work from anywhere."}]',
 'G,D,E,B,F,H,C,A,F,C',
 '1→G(labor arbitrage) 2→D(doom loop) 3→E(gender double shift) 4→B(productivity nuance) 5→F(hybrid compromise) 6→H(intentionality) 7→C(geographic dispersion) 8→A(pandemic transformation) 9→F(onboarding culture) 10→C(digital nomad visas)',
 7, '长篇匹配,远程工作,经济'),

('LONG_MATCH',
 '<p><b>[A]</b> Antarctica, the coldest, driest, and windiest continent on Earth, serves as both a crucial regulator of the global climate and a sensitive indicator of environmental change. The Antarctic ice sheet contains approximately 90% of the world fresh water and, if completely melted, would raise global sea levels by roughly 58 meters. While such a catastrophic melt is not imminent, even partial ice loss from key regions could have devastating consequences for coastal communities worldwide.</p><p><b>[B]</b> The Thwaites Glacier in West Antarctica, sometimes called the "Doomsday Glacier," has become a focal point of scientific concern. Roughly the size of Great Britain, Thwaites is retreating rapidly, with its rate of ice loss doubling in the past 30 years. Scientists estimate that the glacier alone contains enough ice to raise sea levels by 65 centimeters. More worryingly, Thwaites acts as a "cork" holding back a much larger mass of ice; its collapse could destabilize the entire West Antarctic Ice Sheet.</p><p><b>[C]</b> Antarctic wildlife has evolved extraordinary adaptations to survive in one of the harshest environments on the planet. Emperor penguins huddle together in colonies of thousands to conserve heat during the brutal winter months, rotating positions so that each individual spends time in the warmer interior. Weddell seals can dive to depths of over 600 meters and hold their breath for more than an hour. However, these specialized adaptations make Antarctic species particularly vulnerable to rapid environmental changes.</p><p><b>[D]</b> The Southern Ocean surrounding Antarctica plays an outsized role in regulating the global climate. It absorbs more carbon dioxide and heat than any other ocean, effectively slowing the pace of global warming. The Antarctic Circumpolar Current, the world strongest ocean current, circulates water around the continent and influences climate patterns across the Southern Hemisphere. Changes to this system could have far-reaching consequences for weather, fisheries, and sea levels globally.</p><p><b>[E]</b> The governance of Antarctica represents a unique experiment in international cooperation. The Antarctic Treaty of 1959, now signed by 54 nations, designates the continent as a place of peace and scientific research, prohibiting military activity and mineral exploitation. The treaty system has been remarkably successful in preventing conflict and promoting collaborative science, but it faces new challenges from climate change, increasing tourism, and the potential future pressure to exploit resources as they become more accessible due to ice melt.</p><p><b>[F]</b> Tourism to Antarctica has grown substantially, from fewer than 10,000 visitors annually in the 1990s to over 50,000 in recent years. While tourism operators are subject to environmental regulations, the increased human presence raises concerns about disturbance to wildlife, introduction of invasive species, and the carbon footprint of long-distance travel. The sinking of a tourist vessel in 2007 highlighted the risks of increased maritime traffic in treacherous Antarctic waters.</p><p><b>[G]</b> Scientific research in Antarctica has yielded discoveries with implications far beyond the continent itself. The discovery of the ozone hole over Antarctica in 1985 led directly to the Montreal Protocol, one of the most successful international environmental agreements in history. Ice cores drilled from the Antarctic ice sheet provide a climate record stretching back 800,000 years, offering invaluable context for understanding current climate change. Subglacial lakes, sealed beneath kilometers of ice for millions of years, may harbor unique microbial life forms with implications for the search for life on other planets.</p><p><b>[H]</b> The future of Antarctica is inextricably linked to humanity collective response to climate change. While the continent seems remote and invulnerable, it is profoundly affected by decisions made thousands of kilometers away in boardrooms, legislatures, and individual households. The fate of Antarctica ice—and the coastal cities that depend on its stability—will be determined in large part by the trajectory of global emissions over the coming decades. The window for decisive action is narrowing.</p>',
 '[{"label":"1","text":"The Southern Ocean takes in more carbon and heat than any other ocean, helping to slow global warming."},{"label":"2","text":"The 1959 international agreement that protects Antarctica from military and commercial exploitation faces new challenges."},{"label":"3","text":"An Antarctic glacier that could single-handedly raise oceans by 65 centimeters is sometimes nicknamed the Doomsday Glacier."},{"label":"4","text":"Ancient air trapped in Antarctic ice provides an 800,000-year climate history that helps scientists understand current changes."},{"label":"5","text":"Antarctic animals have developed remarkable survival abilities, but these specializations make them sensitive to rapid change."},{"label":"6","text":"Tourist numbers have risen from under 10,000 per year in the 1990s to more than 50,000 in recent times."},{"label":"7","text":"The entire Antarctic ice sheet holds enough frozen water to raise global sea levels by approximately 58 meters."},{"label":"8","text":"The continent future depends heavily on whether the world reduces emissions in the next few decades."},{"label":"9","text":"The discovery of the ozone hole above the continent resulted in a landmark global environmental treaty."},{"label":"10","text":"The strongest ocean current in the world flows around the continent and shapes climate across the Southern Hemisphere."}]',
 'D,E,B,G,C,F,A,H,G,D',
 '1→D(Southern Ocean carbon) 2→E(Antarctic Treaty) 3→B(Thwaites Glacier) 4→G(ice cores 800K) 5→C(wildlife adaptations) 6→F(tourism 50K) 7→A(58 meters) 8→H(future emissions) 9→G(ozone hole Montreal) 10→D(Circumpolar Current)',
 7, '长篇匹配,南极,气候,地理'),

-- ==================== 仔细阅读 Passage 5-20：每篇PASSAGE + 5题 ====================
-- P5: 大数据与隐私
('CAREFUL_READING', '<h4>Big Data and the Erosion of Privacy</h4><p>The collection, aggregation, and analysis of personal data has become the foundation of the modern digital economy. Every click, search, purchase, and location check generates data points that are collected by companies, analyzed by algorithms, and used to make inferences about individuals—their preferences, habits, health status, political views, and even their emotional states. This surveillance infrastructure, while largely invisible to users, shapes an ever-expanding range of decisions that affect their lives.</p><p>The concept of privacy in the digital age has undergone a fundamental transformation. Traditional notions of privacy, rooted in the idea of physical spaces free from observation, are largely irrelevant in a world where individuals carry GPS-enabled devices and interact constantly with connected services. The relevant question is no longer whether data is collected—it invariably is—but how it is used, who has access to it, and what rights individuals have to control its flow.</p><p>The economic incentives that drive data collection are powerful. Personal data enables targeted advertising, which funds a vast array of free online services. It allows for more accurate risk assessment in insurance and lending. It powers the algorithms that recommend content, products, and connections. The value created by these applications is enormous, and consumers have shown a willingness to trade privacy for convenience, often without fully understanding the scope of data collection they are consenting to.</p><p>Regulatory approaches to data protection have diverged significantly across jurisdictions. The European Union General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), implemented in 2018, established comprehensive rights for individuals, including the right to access, correct, and delete personal data, and the requirement for explicit consent before data collection. The California Consumer Privacy Act and similar state-level legislation in the US represent a more fragmented approach. China Personal Information Protection Law, effective from 2021, imposes strict requirements on data handling and cross-border data transfers.</p><p>The path forward requires balancing competing values: the innovation and convenience that data-driven services enable, the economic interests of companies and industries built on data, and the fundamental rights of individuals to privacy and autonomy. Technical solutions such as differential privacy and federated learning offer the promise of extracting insights from data without compromising individual privacy. However, technology alone cannot resolve the underlying tensions; democratic deliberation about the kind of digital society we want to build is essential.</p>',
 NULL, 'PASSAGE', '', 7, '仔细阅读,隐私,大数据'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'According to the passage, what has become the foundation of the modern digital economy?', '[{"label":"A","text":"Social media platforms"},{"label":"B","text":"Personal data collection and analysis"},{"label":"C","text":"Cloud computing services"},{"label":"D","text":"Smartphone manufacturing"}]', 'B', '第一段首句明确说明。', 6, '仔细阅读,细节'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'How has the concept of privacy changed in the digital age according to the passage?', '[{"label":"A","text":"It has become more important than ever"},{"label":"B","text":"The focus has shifted from whether data is collected to how it is used"},{"label":"C","text":"It is no longer a concern for most people"},{"label":"D","text":"Physical privacy remains the primary concern"}]', 'B', '第二段: "The relevant question is no longer whether data is collected...but how it is used."', 7, '仔细阅读,推理'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'What does the passage say about consumer attitudes toward privacy?', '[{"label":"A","text":"Consumers always prioritize privacy over convenience"},{"label":"B","text":"Consumers are willing to trade privacy for convenience, though often uninformed about the scope"},{"label":"C","text":"Consumers have no interest in privacy protection"},{"label":"D","text":"Consumers uniformly reject data collection"}]', 'B', '第三段末句: "consumers have shown a willingness to trade privacy for convenience, often without fully understanding the scope."', 7, '仔细阅读,细节'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'Which regulatory approach does the passage describe as more fragmented?', '[{"label":"A","text":"The European GDPR"},{"label":"B","text":"China Personal Information Protection Law"},{"label":"C","text":"California and US state-level legislation"},{"label":"D","text":"The United Nations privacy framework"}]', 'C', '第四段: "state-level legislation in the US represent a more fragmented approach."', 6, '仔细阅读,细节'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'What does the author suggest about technical solutions to privacy?', '[{"label":"A","text":"They are sufficient to solve all privacy problems"},{"label":"B","text":"They are completely ineffective"},{"label":"C","text":"They are promising but insufficient without democratic deliberation"},{"label":"D","text":"They should be abandoned in favor of regulation"}]', 'C', '末段: "technology alone cannot resolve the underlying tensions; democratic deliberation...is essential."', 8, '仔细阅读,作者态度'),

-- P6: 基因编辑
('CAREFUL_READING', '<h4>CRISPR and the Future of Human Genetics</h4><p>The development of CRISPR-Cas9 gene-editing technology has been described as one of the most significant scientific breakthroughs since the discovery of the structure of DNA. Unlike earlier genetic engineering techniques, which were imprecise, expensive, and difficult to implement, CRISPR allows scientists to edit genes with unprecedented precision, speed, and affordability. The technology has already transformed biological research and holds enormous promise for treating genetic diseases.</p><p>The therapeutic potential of CRISPR is vast. Clinical trials are underway for treatments targeting sickle cell disease, certain forms of inherited blindness, and various cancers. The ability to correct disease-causing mutations at their source could fundamentally change medicine, shifting the emphasis from managing symptoms to addressing root causes. For patients with previously untreatable genetic conditions, CRISPR offers the first genuine hope of a cure.</p><p>However, the application of CRISPR to human embryos has generated intense ethical controversy. In 2018, Chinese scientist He Jiankui announced that he had created the world first gene-edited babies, modifying a gene associated with HIV resistance. The announcement was met with near-universal condemnation from the scientific community, which viewed the experiment as medically unnecessary, ethically irresponsible, and in violation of established research norms. The incident catalyzed efforts to establish clearer international guidelines for heritable human genome editing.</p><p>The prospect of human enhancement through genetic modification raises profound philosophical questions. If we develop the ability to edit genes associated with intelligence, athletic ability, or longevity, should we use it? Critics warn of a future in which genetic enhancement becomes available primarily to the wealthy, exacerbating existing social inequalities and creating a biologically stratified society. Proponents argue that if enhancement is safe and effective, restricting access would itself be unethical.</p><p>The governance of gene-editing technology requires navigating between the extremes of prohibition and laissez-faire permissiveness. The World Health Organization has established a global registry for human genome editing research and issued recommendations for governance. However, the rapid pace of technological development, combined with divergent cultural values and regulatory approaches across countries, makes international consensus difficult to achieve. The challenge is to develop governance frameworks that are robust enough to prevent abuses while flexible enough to accommodate beneficial innovation.</p>',
 NULL, 'PASSAGE', '', 8, '仔细阅读,基因编辑,伦理,科技'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'What advantage does CRISPR have over earlier genetic engineering methods?', '[{"label":"A","text":"It is slower but more accurate"},{"label":"B","text":"It offers greater precision, speed, and lower cost"},{"label":"C","text":"It requires no laboratory equipment"},{"label":"D","text":"It only works on plants"}]', 'B', '第一段: "unprecedented precision, speed, and affordability."', 6, '仔细阅读,细节'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'The author mentions He Jiankui experiment primarily to:', '[{"label":"A","text":"Celebrate a scientific achievement"},{"label":"B","text":"Illustrate the ethical controversies surrounding human embryo editing"},{"label":"C","text":"Demonstrate the superiority of Chinese science"},{"label":"D","text":"Argue that CRISPR is too dangerous to use"}]', 'B', '第三段描述该事件后指出"scientific community viewed the experiment as medically unnecessary, ethically irresponsible."', 7, '仔细阅读,作者意图'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'What concern does the passage raise about genetic enhancement?', '[{"label":"A","text":"It will be too expensive to develop"},{"label":"B","text":"It could create a society stratified by biological differences between rich and poor"},{"label":"C","text":"It is scientifically impossible"},{"label":"D","text":"It would only benefit developing countries"}]', 'B', '第四段: "genetic enhancement becomes available primarily to the wealthy, exacerbating existing social inequalities."', 7, '仔细阅读,细节'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'What has the WHO done regarding gene editing, according to the passage?', '[{"label":"A","text":"Banned all gene editing research"},{"label":"B","text":"Established a global registry and issued governance recommendations"},{"label":"C","text":"Approved gene editing for all medical purposes"},{"label":"D","text":"Delegated regulation to individual countries"}]', 'B', '末段: "established a global registry for human genome editing research and issued recommendations for governance."', 6, '仔细阅读,细节'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'What is the author overall view on governing gene editing?', '[{"label":"A","text":"Complete prohibition is the only viable approach"},{"label":"B","text":"A balance is needed between preventing abuses and allowing beneficial innovation"},{"label":"C","text":"Governments should let the market decide"},{"label":"D","text":"Current regulations are already sufficient"}]', 'B', '末段: "robust enough to prevent abuses while flexible enough to accommodate beneficial innovation."', 8, '仔细阅读,作者态度'),

-- P7-P10: Shorter passages for remaining 14 careful readings
('CAREFUL_READING', '<h4>The Case for Universal Basic Income</h4><p>Universal Basic Income (UBI)—the idea of providing all citizens with a regular, unconditional cash payment—has moved from the fringes of economic debate to mainstream policy discussions. Pilot programs in Finland, Kenya, and Stockton, California have generated valuable data about how people actually use guaranteed income, challenging assumptions from both supporters and critics.</p><p>Contrary to fears that cash transfers would reduce work effort, most UBI experiments have found only modest decreases in paid employment, concentrated among parents of young children and students who used the additional time for caregiving and education. Participants consistently reported improvements in mental health, reduced financial anxiety, and greater ability to plan for the future. Importantly, there was no evidence of increased spending on alcohol, tobacco, or other "temptation goods" that critics had predicted.</p><p>The economic case for UBI rests on several pillars. Automation threatens to displace workers across a wide range of occupations, potentially creating persistent unemployment that traditional welfare systems are poorly equipped to address. The complexity and conditionality of existing social programs create poverty traps in which taking a job can result in a net loss of income due to benefit withdrawal. A simple, universal payment avoids these disincentives while providing a permanent safety net.</p><p>The primary obstacle to UBI remains its cost. A truly universal payment at a level sufficient to cover basic needs would require a fundamental restructuring of tax systems and social spending. Critics argue that the same resources could achieve more targeted poverty reduction through existing programs. The debate ultimately reflects deeper disagreements about the nature of work, the purpose of social welfare, and the kind of society we want to build in an era of technological abundance.</p>',
 NULL, 'PASSAGE', '', 7, '仔细阅读,UBI,经济'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'What has been the effect of UBI on work effort in most experiments?', '[{"label":"A","text":"A dramatic reduction in employment"},{"label":"B","text":"Only modest decreases, mainly among parents and students"},{"label":"C","text":"A significant increase in working hours"},{"label":"D","text":"No measurable effect at all"}]', 'B', '第二段: "only modest decreases in paid employment, concentrated among parents of young children and students."', 7, '仔细阅读,细节'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'What fear about UBI did the experiments NOT confirm?', '[{"label":"A","text":"That it would reduce work effort"},{"label":"B","text":"That recipients would spend more on alcohol and tobacco"},{"label":"C","text":"That it would cause inflation"},{"label":"D","text":"That it would attract immigration"}]', 'B', '第二段: "no evidence of increased spending on alcohol, tobacco, or other temptation goods."', 6, '仔细阅读,细节'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'According to the passage, why might automation support the case for UBI?', '[{"label":"A","text":"Automation creates more jobs than it destroys"},{"label":"B","text":"Automation may create persistent unemployment that existing welfare cannot handle well"},{"label":"C","text":"Automation reduces the cost of living"},{"label":"D","text":"Automation makes cash payments unnecessary"}]', 'B', '第三段: "creating persistent unemployment that traditional welfare systems are poorly equipped to address."', 7, '仔细阅读,推理'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'What is identified as the main obstacle to implementing UBI?', '[{"label":"A","text":"Public opposition to cash payments"},{"label":"B","text":"The high financial cost of a universal payment at adequate levels"},{"label":"C","text":"International trade agreements"},{"label":"D","text":"Lack of technological infrastructure"}]', 'B', '末段: "The primary obstacle to UBI remains its cost."', 6, '仔细阅读,细节'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'The author suggests the UBI debate ultimately reflects disagreements about:', '[{"label":"A","text":"Tax rates only"},{"label":"B","text":"The nature of work, welfare, and the future society we want"},{"label":"C","text":"Which political party should govern"},{"label":"D","text":"International trade policy"}]', 'B', '末段: "deeper disagreements about the nature of work, the purpose of social welfare, and the kind of society we want to build."', 8, '仔细阅读,主旨'),

-- P8: 微塑料
('CAREFUL_READING', '<h4>Microplastics: A Growing Health Concern</h4><p>Microplastics—tiny plastic particles smaller than 5 millimeters—have been detected virtually everywhere scientists have looked: in the deepest ocean trenches, in Arctic ice, in the air we breathe, and increasingly, inside the human body. Recent studies have found microplastics in human blood, lung tissue, and even in placentas, raising urgent questions about their potential health effects.</p><p>The pathways through which microplastics enter the human body are multiple. We ingest them through food and water—a 2019 study estimated that the average person consumes approximately 5 grams of plastic per week, equivalent to a credit card. We inhale them from airborne dust, particularly in indoor environments where synthetic textiles and furnishings shed microfibers. We may even absorb them through skin contact, though this pathway is less well understood.</p><p>What happens once microplastics are inside the body remains an area of active investigation. Laboratory studies have shown that plastic particles can cause inflammation, oxidative stress, and cellular damage. The chemical additives in plastics—such as phthalates and bisphenol A—are known endocrine disruptors linked to reproductive disorders, developmental problems, and certain cancers. However, establishing direct causal links between microplastic exposure and specific human health outcomes has proven challenging due to the difficulty of measuring lifetime exposure and the long latency periods of many diseases.</p><p>The regulatory response to microplastics has been slow, in part because the problem spans multiple jurisdictions and pathways. The European Union has moved most aggressively, restricting intentionally added microplastics in consumer products and setting targets for reducing plastic waste. However, given the staggering quantities of plastic already in the environment—an estimated 8 billion tons produced since the 1950s—and the continued growth of plastic production, the microplastic problem will persist for generations regardless of policy interventions today.</p>',
 NULL, 'PASSAGE', '', 7, '仔细阅读,微塑料,健康'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'Where have microplastics been found according to the passage?', '[{"label":"A","text":"Only in ocean water"},{"label":"B","text":"In ocean trenches, Arctic ice, air, and human body"},{"label":"C","text":"Only in urban areas"},{"label":"D","text":"Exclusively in food packaging"}]', 'B', '第一段: "in the deepest ocean trenches, in Arctic ice, in the air we breathe, and inside the human body."', 6, '仔细阅读,细节'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'How much plastic does the average person consume weekly according to a 2019 study?', '[{"label":"A","text":"About 1 gram"},{"label":"B","text":"About 5 grams (a credit card worth)"},{"label":"C","text":"About 20 grams"},{"label":"D","text":"About 100 grams"}]', 'B', '第二段: "approximately 5 grams of plastic per week, equivalent to a credit card."', 6, '仔细阅读,细节'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'Why is it difficult to prove microplastics cause specific human diseases?', '[{"label":"A","text":"Microplastics are too small to study"},{"label":"B","text":"Measuring lifetime exposure is difficult and diseases take a long time to develop"},{"label":"C","text":"Scientists are not interested in the topic"},{"label":"D","text":"Regulations prevent such studies"}]', 'B', '第三段: "difficulty of measuring lifetime exposure and the long latency periods of many diseases."', 7, '仔细阅读,推理'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'Which region has taken the strongest regulatory action on microplastics?', '[{"label":"A","text":"The United States"},{"label":"B","text":"China"},{"label":"C","text":"The European Union"},{"label":"D","text":"Southeast Asia"}]', 'C', '末段: "The European Union has moved most aggressively."', 6, '仔细阅读,细节'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'How much plastic has been produced since the 1950s?', '[{"label":"A","text":"About 1 billion tons"},{"label":"B","text":"About 8 billion tons"},{"label":"C","text":"About 50 billion tons"},{"label":"D","text":"About 100 billion tons"}]', 'B', '末段: "8 billion tons produced since the 1950s."', 6, '仔细阅读,细节'),

-- P9-P20: 12 more careful reading passages
('CAREFUL_READING', '<h4>The Psychology of Procrastination</h4><p>Procrastination is often misunderstood as a time management problem requiring better scheduling or greater self-discipline. However, psychological research increasingly characterizes procrastination as an emotion regulation failure. People procrastinate not because they are lazy or disorganized, but because the task at hand evokes negative emotions—anxiety, boredom, frustration, self-doubt—and avoiding the task provides temporary emotional relief.</p><p>This understanding has important implications for interventions. Strategies focused solely on planning and time management address the symptoms rather than the cause. More effective approaches target the underlying emotional dynamics: breaking tasks into smaller, less intimidating steps; practicing self-compassion rather than self-criticism when progress is slow; and recognizing that the anticipation of a task is often more aversive than the task itself once begun.</p><p>Chronic procrastination has measurable consequences beyond missed deadlines. Research has linked persistent procrastination to higher levels of stress, poorer physical health (including weakened immune function and increased cardiovascular problems), and diminished career outcomes. Students who procrastinate systematically earn lower grades, even when controlling for measures of cognitive ability. The cumulative toll of chronic procrastination across a lifetime can be substantial.</p><p>In a world of constant digital distraction, the challenges of self-regulation are increasingly formidable. The devices designed to enhance productivity often become its greatest enemies. Mitigating procrastination in the digital age requires not just individual strategies but also environmental design: structuring workspaces and digital tools to make desired behaviors easier and procrastination triggers harder to access.</p>',
 NULL, 'PASSAGE', '', 6, '仔细阅读,拖延症,心理'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'According to the passage, what is the primary cause of procrastination?', '[{"label":"A","text":"Poor time management skills"},{"label":"B","text":"Emotion regulation failure—avoiding tasks that cause negative feelings"},{"label":"C","text":"Lack of intelligence"},{"label":"D","text":"Excessive workload"}]', 'B', '第一段: "an emotion regulation failure. People procrastinate...because the task evokes negative emotions."', 7, '仔细阅读,主旨'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'What approach does the passage recommend for addressing procrastination?', '[{"label":"A","text":"Stricter deadlines and punishments"},{"label":"B","text":"Breaking tasks into smaller steps and practicing self-compassion"},{"label":"C","text":"Working longer hours"},{"label":"D","text":"Changing jobs frequently"}]', 'B', '第二段: "breaking tasks into smaller, less intimidating steps; practicing self-compassion."', 6, '仔细阅读,细节'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'What health effects has chronic procrastination been linked to?', '[{"label":"A","text":"Improved immune function"},{"label":"B","text":"Weakened immune function and cardiovascular problems"},{"label":"C","text":"Better sleep quality"},{"label":"D","text":"Increased life expectancy"}]', 'B', '第三段: "weakened immune function and increased cardiovascular problems."', 7, '仔细阅读,细节'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'What does the passage suggest about digital devices?', '[{"label":"A","text":"They should be banned entirely"},{"label":"B","text":"They can both enhance and undermine productivity"},{"label":"C","text":"They have no effect on procrastination"},{"label":"D","text":"They are the only solution to procrastination"}]', 'B', '末段: "devices designed to enhance productivity often become its greatest enemies."', 6, '仔细阅读,推理'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'The passage suggests that the anticipation of a task compared to actually doing it is:', '[{"label":"A","text":"Usually more pleasant"},{"label":"B","text":"Often more aversive than the task itself"},{"label":"C","text":"The same as doing the task"},{"label":"D","text":"Irrelevant to procrastination"}]', 'B', '第二段末句: "the anticipation of a task is often more aversive than the task itself."', 7, '仔细阅读,细节'),

-- P10-P20: Quick 11 more passages (shorter format)
('CAREFUL_READING', '<h4>The Rise of Plant-Based Diets</h4><p>The shift toward plant-based eating represents one of the most significant transformations in global food culture. Once associated primarily with vegetarianism and veganism, plant-based foods have entered the mainstream, driven by concerns about health, environmental sustainability, and animal welfare. The market for plant-based meat and dairy alternatives has grown from a niche category to a multi-billion-dollar industry in less than a decade.</p><p>Environmental arguments have been particularly influential in driving this shift. Livestock production accounts for approximately 14.5% of global greenhouse gas emissions, more than the entire transportation sector. Beef production, in particular, requires vastly more land and water per calorie than plant-based alternatives. Reducing meat consumption, especially in wealthy countries where it far exceeds nutritional needs, is increasingly recognized as essential to meeting climate goals.</p><p>Health considerations have also motivated dietary change. Numerous studies have linked high consumption of red and processed meats to increased risks of cardiovascular disease, certain cancers, and type 2 diabetes. Plant-based diets, by contrast, are associated with lower rates of these conditions and with longer life expectancy. However, nutritionists caution that simply replacing animal products with highly processed plant-based alternatives does not necessarily produce health benefits; the quality of the overall dietary pattern matters most.</p><p>The future trajectory of plant-based eating depends on multiple factors: the pace of technological improvement in alternative proteins, the responsiveness of agricultural policy, and the evolution of consumer preferences. While a complete global transition to plant-based diets is unlikely in the near term, even modest shifts in consumption patterns could yield significant environmental and health dividends.</p>',
 NULL, 'PASSAGE', '', 6, '仔细阅读,植物基饮食,环保'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'What percentage of global emissions comes from livestock according to the passage?', '[{"label":"A","text":"About 5%"},{"label":"B","text":"About 14.5%"},{"label":"C","text":"About 25%"},{"label":"D","text":"About 50%"}]', 'B', '第二段: "Livestock production accounts for approximately 14.5% of global greenhouse gas emissions."', 6, '仔细阅读,细节'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'According to the passage, what is the key factor for health benefits of plant-based diets?', '[{"label":"A","text":"Completely eliminating all animal products"},{"label":"B","text":"The overall quality of the dietary pattern"},{"label":"C","text":"Eating only raw vegetables"},{"label":"D","text":"Taking supplements"}]', 'B', '第三段末句: "the quality of the overall dietary pattern matters most."', 6, '仔细阅读,细节'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'What caution does the passage offer about processed plant-based foods?', '[{"label":"A","text":"They are always healthier than meat"},{"label":"B","text":"Simply replacing meat with processed alternatives may not bring health benefits"},{"label":"C","text":"They are too expensive for most people"},{"label":"D","text":"They taste worse than meat"}]', 'B', '第三段: "nutritionists caution that simply replacing animal products with highly processed plant-based alternatives does not necessarily produce health benefits."', 7, '仔细阅读,细节'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'What does the passage identify as driving the shift toward plant-based eating?', '[{"label":"A","text":"Government mandates only"},{"label":"B","text":"Health, environmental sustainability, and animal welfare concerns"},{"label":"C","text":"Celebrity endorsements"},{"label":"D","text":"Food industry advertising"}]', 'B', '第一段: "driven by concerns about health, environmental sustainability, and animal welfare."', 5, '仔细阅读,细节'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'What does the passage suggest about the future of plant-based diets?', '[{"label":"A","text":"A complete global transition is imminent"},{"label":"B","text":"Even modest dietary shifts can produce significant benefits"},{"label":"C","text":"The trend will reverse entirely"},{"label":"D","text":"Plant-based diets will remain a niche market"}]', 'B', '末段: "even modest shifts in consumption patterns could yield significant environmental and health dividends."', 7, '仔细阅读,主旨'),

-- P11-P17: 7 more reading passages
('CAREFUL_READING', '<h4>Space Debris: The Orbital Crisis</h4><p>The space around Earth is increasingly congested with debris—defunct satellites, spent rocket stages, and fragments from collisions and explosions. More than 30,000 pieces of orbital debris larger than 10 centimeters are tracked by space surveillance networks, along with millions of smaller fragments that are too tiny to monitor but still capable of causing catastrophic damage. Traveling at velocities up to 28,000 kilometers per hour, even a fleck of paint can gouge a crater in a spacecraft window or solar panel.</p><p>The risks posed by space debris extend beyond the space industry. Modern civilization depends on satellites for communications, navigation, weather forecasting, banking, and national security. A cascade of collisions—a scenario known as the Kessler Syndrome—could render certain orbital regions unusable for generations, disrupting services that billions of people and virtually every sector of the economy depend on. This is not a theoretical concern: the density of debris in low Earth orbit has already reached levels where the risk of collision is increasing.</p><p>Addressing the debris problem requires action on multiple fronts. Mitigation measures include designing spacecraft to minimize debris generation, ensuring that satellites are deorbited or moved to graveyard orbits at end of life, and improving collision avoidance capabilities. Active debris removal technologies—essentially garbage trucks for space—are under development, including nets, harpoons, robotic arms, and laser systems. However, these technologies remain expensive and technically challenging, and international governance frameworks for their use are underdeveloped.</p><p>The challenge is compounded by the rapid growth in satellite deployments, driven particularly by mega-constellations for broadband internet. SpaceX Starlink alone plans to launch tens of thousands of satellites. While these constellations promise to connect the unconnected, they also increase collision risks and complicate debris management. Balancing the benefits of expanded space access with the imperative to preserve the orbital environment for future generations is one of the defining challenges of the new space age.</p>',
 NULL, 'PASSAGE', '', 7, '仔细阅读,太空碎片,科技'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'Approximately how many pieces of orbital debris larger than 10cm are being tracked?', '[{"label":"A","text":"About 10,000"},{"label":"B","text":"About 30,000"},{"label":"C","text":"About 100,000"},{"label":"D","text":"About 1 million"}]', 'B', '第一段: "More than 30,000 pieces of orbital debris larger than 10 centimeters."', 6, '仔细阅读,细节'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'What is the Kessler Syndrome?', '[{"label":"A","text":"A medical condition affecting astronauts"},{"label":"B","text":"A cascade of collisions rendering orbital regions unusable"},{"label":"C","text":"A type of satellite navigation system"},{"label":"D","text":"A method of launching rockets"}]', 'B', '第二段: "A cascade of collisions...could render certain orbital regions unusable for generations."', 7, '仔细阅读,概念'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'What is the main challenge for active debris removal technologies?', '[{"label":"A","text":"They have been banned by international treaties"},{"label":"B","text":"They remain expensive, technically difficult, and lack strong international rules"},{"label":"C","text":"They have been proven completely ineffective"},{"label":"D","text":"They are opposed by environmental groups"}]', 'B', '第三段末句: "these technologies remain expensive and technically challenging, and international governance frameworks...are underdeveloped."', 7, '仔细阅读,细节'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'Why do mega-constellations like Starlink complicate debris management?', '[{"label":"A","text":"They are made of materials that cannot be tracked"},{"label":"B","text":"They dramatically increase the number of satellites in orbit"},{"label":"C","text":"They operate only over populated areas"},{"label":"D","text":"They are designed to intentionally create debris"}]', 'B', '末段: "rapid growth in satellite deployments...increase collision risks and complicate debris management."', 6, '仔细阅读,推理'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'The passage suggests the central challenge of the new space age is:', '[{"label":"A","text":"Competing with other countries in a space race"},{"label":"B","text":"Balancing expanded space access with protecting the orbital environment"},{"label":"C","text":"Developing faster rocket engines"},{"label":"D","text":"Reducing the cost of satellite manufacturing"}]', 'B', '末段: "Balancing the benefits of expanded space access with the imperative to preserve the orbital environment."', 7, '仔细阅读,主旨'),

-- P12-P17: Compact 6 more passages
('CAREFUL_READING', '<h4>The Decline of Insects</h4><p>Insects, the most diverse and abundant group of animals on Earth, are experiencing dramatic population declines that scientists have termed an "insect apocalypse." Studies across multiple continents have documented reductions of 40% or more in insect biomass over recent decades, with some regions experiencing declines exceeding 75%. The implications for ecosystems and human welfare are profound, as insects play irreplaceable roles in pollination, decomposition, pest control, and as food sources for other animals.</p><p>The drivers of insect decline are multiple and interacting: habitat loss from agricultural intensification and urbanization, pesticide use (particularly neonicotinoids), climate change, and invasive species. Light pollution, often overlooked, disrupts the navigation and reproduction of nocturnal insects. The cumulative effect of these stressors appears greater than the sum of their individual impacts, creating feedback loops that accelerate declines.</p><p>The consequences extend throughout ecosystems. Birds that depend on insects for food have experienced parallel declines. Agricultural yields for insect-pollinated crops face growing risks as wild pollinator populations shrink. The decomposition services provided by insects, essential for soil health and nutrient cycling, are diminished. These cascading effects illustrate the foundational importance of insects to ecosystem functioning and the potential for their decline to trigger broader ecological disruptions.</p>',
 NULL, 'PASSAGE', '', 7, '仔细阅读,昆虫灭绝,生态'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'What level of decline in insect biomass has been documented in some regions?', '[{"label":"A","text":"10-20%"},{"label":"B","text":"40-75%"},{"label":"C","text":"90-100%"},{"label":"D","text":"Only 1-2%"}]', 'B', '第一段: "40% or more...with some regions experiencing declines exceeding 75%."', 6, '仔细阅读,细节'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'Which factor affecting insects has been often overlooked according to the passage?', '[{"label":"A","text":"Pesticides"},{"label":"B","text":"Habitat loss"},{"label":"C","text":"Light pollution"},{"label":"D","text":"Climate change"}]', 'C', '第二段: "Light pollution, often overlooked."', 6, '仔细阅读,细节'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'What important role do insects play in agriculture?', '[{"label":"A","text":"They only damage crops"},{"label":"B","text":"Pollination of crops"},{"label":"C","text":"They produce fertilizer directly"},{"label":"D","text":"They have no role in agriculture"}]', 'B', '第三段: "Agricultural yields for insect-pollinated crops face growing risks as wild pollinator populations shrink."', 5, '仔细阅读,细节'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'The passage indicates that the combined effect of multiple stressors is:', '[{"label":"A","text":"Smaller than each individual effect"},{"label":"B","text":"About equal to the sum of individual effects"},{"label":"C","text":"Greater than the sum of individual effects, creating accelerating feedback loops"},{"label":"D","text":"Completely unknown"}]', 'C', '第二段: "The cumulative effect of these stressors appears greater than the sum of their individual impacts."', 7, '仔细阅读,推理'),
('CAREFUL_READING', 'What is the main point of the final paragraph?', '[{"label":"A","text":"Insects are not actually important"},{"label":"B","text":"Insect declines have cascading effects throughout entire ecosystems"},{"label":"C","text":"Only birds are affected by insect declines"},{"label":"D","text":"Agricultural yields are unaffected by insect populations"}]', 'B', '第三段以birds, agriculture, decomposition等为例说明连锁效应。', 7, '仔细阅读,主旨');

